You grade a report on what lies around a property in Berlin, written for someone who thinks
of buying or renting it. The report is a draft: it cites the objects it is about by their IDs
in square brackets, and code later replaces the IDs with sources. Below are the objects that
were checked near the property, how the investigation of each ended, and the sources that
failed.

<objects>
{% for map_object in map_objects %}
{% set finding = findings.get(map_object.id) %}
- {{ map_object.id }}, {{ map_object.rounded_distance_m() }} m from the property{% if map_object.id in plans %}, a development plan{% endif %}: {% if finding is none %}not investigated{% elif finding.outcome == "identified" %}identified as {{ finding.identified_type }}, status {{ finding.status or "not known" }}{% else %}could not be identified{% endif %}

{% endfor %}
{% if nothing_found %}
No object of the checked kinds was found near the property.
{% endif %}
</objects>

<failures>
{{ failures | join(", ") if failures else "No source failed." }}
</failures>

<report>
{{ draft }}
</report>

Answer four questions, each yes, no or unknown, with a short reason. For a no, quote the
words of the report that are wrong, or name what is missing. Answer unknown only if you
cannot tell from the report and the lists above.

1. explains_meaning: For each identified object, does the report give its status, or that its
   status is not known, and, if its type's meaning below names an effect on people nearby, say
   what it can mean for someone who lives at the property, instead of only repeating what was
   found about it? For an object whose type shows no effect, such as commercial, one short
   sentence with what it is and how far away is enough, without its status. An object of type
   other needs, instead of an effect, that it is not known whether it can affect people nearby.
   A development plan needs its legal status and whether building has started there, even if its
   type shows no effect. A closed object whose type names an effect needs its status instead of
   that effect, and may say that it brings none of it. An object that could not be identified or
   was not investigated needs no explanation. A status that differs from the list counts under
   question 4, not here. If no object needs anything, answer yes.
2. lists_unclear_objects: Does the report give each object that could not be identified and
   each object that was not investigated at its end, with its distance, or at 0 m that the
   property lies in its area or right next to it, and say which it was? If there is no such
   object, answer yes.
3. tells_failures: For each failed source, does the report say that its part is missing,
   and never that nothing was found there? Saying that an object could not be identified or
   was not investigated is not saying that nothing was found. If no source failed, answer
   yes.
4. fits_type: Does what the report says each object can mean fit its type and status in the list
   above? Giving an object that could not be identified or was not investigated a meaning, as if
   its type were known, is a no, while naming the kind of place the map shows, such as an
   industrial area, or what a plan's data name, such as the uses it allows, is not a meaning.
   Treating a status that is not known as known, such as saying that the site is in use, is a no
   too, and so is giving a status in the list as not known. A plan's status says whether
   building has started there; its legal status, such as in force, is another fact, and giving
   it is not treating that status as known, but giving it as the reason that building has or has
   not started is a no. A meaning fits only if it is of the kind that its type's meaning below
   gives, and fits the status: what an object in use brings; for one under construction, planned
   or not known to be in use, what it can bring once in use; for a closed object, only that it
   brings none of it. The type other gives none: any effect for it, or saying that its type
   shows no effect or that it is harmless, is a no. If none of this happens in the report,
   answer yes.

The types mean:
{% for name, text in types.items() %}
- {{ name }}: {{ text }}
{% endfor %}

The statuses mean:
{% for name, text in statuses.items() %}
- {{ name }}: {{ text }}
{% endfor %}
